## Bitcoin Core `test/functional/data/rpc_psbt.json`

`src/core_kat_vectors.zig` reproduces conformance vectors from the Bitcoin
Core project's PSBT RPC functional-test data file (fetched 2026-08 from
`master`):

  Copyright (c) 2009-2010 Satoshi Nakamoto
  Copyright (c) 2009-present The Bitcoin Core developers
  Distributed under the MIT software license, see
  https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT

That is the same "preserve the notice" requirement zig-libs' own MIT code
already carries, owed here to a different author — but MIT asks for the
permission notice as well as the copyright line ("The above copyright notice
and this permission notice shall be included in all copies"), and until
2026-09-09 only the copyright line was here. The MIT text is reproduced at the
end of this file.

No third-party SOURCE CODE is present — only test-case DATA (base64
PSBTs, converted to hex to match this module's existing hex convention; see
`core_kat_vectors.zig`'s own doc comment for exactly which of the JSON's
`invalid`/`invalid_with_msg`/`valid`/`finalizer`/`extractor` arrays are
vendored, which entries are skipped as byte-identical BIP174 duplicates, and
what each vendored entry's disposition is (accepted as an intentional,
documented scope cut vs. rejected) — driven by `core_kat_test.zig`.

Two things worth flagging so a future reader doesn't have to re-derive them:
first, Core's own `finalizer`/`extractor` entries turned out to be
byte-identical to `kat_vectors.zig`'s BIP174 Finalizer/Extractor worked
example already vendored above (Core's functional test reuses BIP174's own
example verbatim rather than adding a new spend shape) — not re-vendored a
second time, to avoid duplicating an anchor. Second, working through Core's
`invalid`/`invalid_with_msg` sets surfaced one genuine BIP174 conformance gap
this module had (a present `PSBT_GLOBAL_VERSION` was never checked to
actually be `0`, per BIP174 §"Version 0") — now fixed in `root.zig`, with
Core's own vector as the external oracle. See `core_kat_test.zig`'s doc
comment for the full account, including which of Core's vectors are BIP370/
BIP371/MuSig2 content this module deliberately doesn't validate (not a bug).

